  <bpmn2:collaboration id="collaboration_1" name="Default Collaboration">
    <bpmn2:participant id="participant_QWlG2w" name="Default Process" processRef="process_1"/>
    <bpmn2:participant id="participant_q3xuzA" name="Customer Order Enquiry" processRef="process_mg4eUw">
      <bpmn2:documentation id="documentation_cFPYqg"><![CDATA[Workflow: Customer Order Enquiry

Use this workflow when a CUSTOMER wants to buy goods FROM US

Typical documents:
- Purchase orders sent to us ("Bestellung", "Auftrag", "Purchase Order", "Order")
- Requests for a quotation ("Anfrage", "Angebotsanfrage", "Request for Quotation")
- Typical signs: order number ("Bestellnummer"), item list with quantities and
  prices, delivery date ("Liefertermin"), and often "Ihre Lieferantennummer"
  (= OUR supplier number at the customer, which confirms that we are the seller).

Note: Customer purchase orders often contain the customer's own general terms
and conditions (delivery terms, retention of title, applicable law).
This does NOT make them supplier documents.

Do NOT use this workflow for:
- Complaints, returns, invoices or payment reminders
- General product questions without intent to buy
- Documents from OUR suppliers, i.e. companies that sell goods TO us
  (e.g. their quotations, order confirmations or invoices addressed to us)]]></bpmn2:documentation>
    </bpmn2:participant>
  </bpmn2:collaboration>
  <bpmn2:process definitionalCollaborationRef="collaboration_1" id="process_1" name="Default Process" processType="Public">
    <bpmn2:documentation id="documentation_0rXwGQ"><![CDATA[This workflow handles a customer order enquiry from submission to shipment. ]]></bpmn2:documentation>
    <bpmn2:extensionElements>
      <open-bpmn:auto-align>true</open-bpmn:auto-align>
    </bpmn2:extensionElements>
    <bpmn2:textAnnotation id="textAnnotation_sYfNQg" textFormat="">
      <bpmn2:documentation id="documentation_wcIyfw"/>
      <bpmn2:text id="text_kiKjBA"><![CDATA[Customer Order Enquiry – Purpose

This workflow handles a customer order enquiry from submission to shipment. First, the enquiry is checked for completeness. If required data is missing, it is sent back for correction. Complete enquiries go through a compliance screening that covers EU regulations, sanctions and dual-use restrictions. Any compliance issue is escalated to a Sales Manager for further review.

Once compliance is cleared, the Sales Department reviews the order. It either asks for resubmission or forwards the order to the warehouse. The warehouse checks availability, then picks and packs the goods that are in stock. A delivery note with an order number is created, followed by a shipping label with a tracking number. Finally, tracking information is sent and the order is marked as shipped.

The goal is to process orders in a compliant, traceable and consistent way, with clear handovers between Sales, Compliance and Warehouse, and an escalation path when problems come up.]]></bpmn2:text>
    </bpmn2:textAnnotation>
  </bpmn2:process>
